      <h2 id="buying-guide">Buying Guide: How to Choose a Website Builder</h2>
      <p>The right website builder matches your design skill, your team size, and your content strategy. Follow this framework:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Designers &amp; agencies:</strong> Start with Webflow. The Designer's visual CSS model, CMS collections, and clean output mean you can hand off maintainable sites to clients without rewriting code. Keep Framer in the toolkit for animation-heavy launches.</li>
        <li><strong>Marketing teams / solo founders:</strong> Start with Framer. The AI layout assistant, Figma-like canvas, and modern interaction model get a great-looking site live in hours. Move to Webflow if you outgrow Framer CMS depth.</li>
        <li><strong>Blogs, publishers, SEO-led sites:</strong> Start with WordPress. The CMS, block editor, taxonomies, and SEO plugins are the category standard. Use Elementor Pro or Gutenberg blocks for landing pages.</li>
        <li><strong>Small businesses, creators, photographers:</strong> Start with Squarespace. Pick any template, swap in your brand assets, and ship. You will not need a designer, and the Fluid Engine lets non-designers tweak layout.</li>
        <li><strong>Absolute beginners on a budget:</strong> Start with Wix Free. Use Wix ADI to generate the first draft, then drag elements to refine. Upgrade to a paid plan to connect a custom domain and remove Wix branding.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Common mistakes to avoid: do not pick Wix if you plan to scale past 50 pages with serious SEO ambitions; do not pick Webflow in week one without budget for the learning curve; and do not pick Squarespace if you need custom code exports or a plugin ecosystem.</p>
      <p>Learn more about our <a href="/how-we-test/">testing methodology</a> and <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Which website builder is best in 2026?</summary>
          <p>The best website builder in 2026 depends on your use case. Webflow is best for professional designers who want pixel-perfect control, a real CMS, and clean code export. Framer is best for interactive marketing sites with modern animations and AI-assisted layout. WordPress is best for blogs, SEO-led publishers, and plugin flexibility. Squarespace is best for small businesses and creators that want polished templates without design work. Wix is best for absolute beginners on a budget who want drag-and-drop everything in one bill.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is Webflow or Framer easier to use?</summary>
          <p>Framer is easier to start with and feels more modern. Its canvas is familiar to Figma users, the AI layout assistant ships usable sections, and animations, scroll-triggered effects, and responsive breakpoints have a gentler learning curve. Webflow has a steeper onboarding because the Designer reflects HTML/CSS mental models (box model, flexbox, grid, classes, combo classes) and the panel-based styling takes weeks to master. Once mastered, Webflow gives cleaner, more predictable production output and a more mature CMS. Designers typically prefer Webflow; marketers and solo founders often prefer Framer.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which website builder has the best SEO?</summary>
          <p>WordPress has the highest SEO ceiling because you control every layer with plugins like Yoast SEO, Rank Math, and Redirection, plus page speed tuning through caching and image optimization. Webflow has the strongest native SEO among SaaS builders — clean semantic HTML, XML sitemaps, robots.txt, metadata per collection item, CDN and SSL by default, and no code bloat from page builders. Framer's SEO is solid but more basic; Squarespace SEO is adequate but less granular; Wix has improved SEO dramatically since switching to full SSR but still lags Webflow and WordPress on technical SEO.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I move my site from one builder to another?</summary>
          <p>Yes, partially. Blog posts and CMS collections can usually be exported as CSV or RSS and imported into the target builder. Pages and designs cannot be migrated faithfully because each builder uses proprietary rendering — you should plan to rebuild the design. Common migrations are Squarespace → Webflow (when you outgrow template constraints), Wix → WordPress (when you need plugin flexibility), and Figma → Framer/Webflow (when a designer hands off files). Budget 2–4 weeks for a migration of a 50-page site, and keep the old site live until redirects are verified.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

